Colour the scroll bars of the browser from QtCurve

A browser draws the scroll bar of a page through GTK, and it asks
the theme for the colours of the #MozillaGtkWidget node chain. It
takes those colours while the trough of the chain holds no ground.
The sheets of QtCurve carried no rule of that chain, so every page
kept the grey pair of the browser.

The GTK 3 sheet now carries the block. A sibling of the scroll bar
section writes it, so the rules of the widget keep every byte. A
node flag holds the block to the GTK 3 dialect, because the browser
reads no GTK 4 sheet. Without the flag, the names of the block
would move the floors of that dialect.

The block states the colours of the Qt style. It holds the ladder
of Style::sliderColors() under each slider key, one step of that
ladder for the pointer, and the groove colour of the bar. Every
groove path of the style paints the groove at step two of a shade
ladder, so one colour serves every setting. The ground takes the
Base seed of the palette. The helper of palette.cpp holds the
derivation, and the block reads it. The block carries colours
alone. A border of the chain paints no pixel of the browser, and an
image of it stands unproven.

assert-browser.sh binds the block to the settings over eleven arms.
assert-alphas.sh now takes a comment of a whole line, because the
selector of a row can start with a number sign.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 9 of 14. The branch holds those fourteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

